The code uses First()! on line 116, which will throw an InvalidOperationException if no assembly contains an icudt.dat resource. Consider using FirstOrDefault() and adding a more descriptive error message to help diagnose which platform/configuration is missing the required resource file. For example: "Failed to find icudt.dat resource in any loaded assembly. Ensure the Uno.ICU package is properly referenced."
```suggestion
					.FirstOrDefault();

				if (stream is null)
				{
					throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to find icudt.dat resource in any loaded assembly. Ensure the Uno.ICU package is properly referenced.");
				}
```

Using `First()` without a default will throw an `InvalidOperationException` if no matching assembly is found. Since this code runs only under `__SKIA__` conditional, the assembly should exist, but the generator code uses the safer pattern of checking for null on reflection results. Consider using `FirstOrDefault()` with a null check or wrapping in a try-catch with a more descriptive error message to be consistent with the defensive programming pattern used elsewhere.
```suggestion
			var assembly = AppDomain.CurrentDomain
				.GetAssemblies()
				.FirstOrDefault(a => a.GetName().Name?.StartsWith("SamplesApp.Skia.", StringComparison.Ordinal) ?? false);

			if (assembly is null)
			{
				throw new InvalidOperationException("Unable to locate a SamplesApp.Skia.* assembly required to initialize ICU data.");
			}
```

Using `First()` will throw an exception if no assembly matching the predicate is found. This could happen in unexpected deployment scenarios. Use `FirstOrDefault()` and handle the null case, or provide a more informative error message if the assembly is not found.
```suggestion
			var assembly = AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies().FirstOrDefault(a => a.GetName().Name?.StartsWith("SamplesApp.Skia.", StringComparison.Ordinal) ?? false);
			if (assembly is null)
			{
				throw new InvalidOperationException("Unable to locate the SamplesApp Skia assembly required for ICU data initialization.");
			}
```

There's no null check on the result of `Marshal.PtrToStringUTF8()`. If `u_errorName` returns a null pointer for some reason, this could lead to issues. Additionally, the log level changed from `Warn` to `Trace` which is a significant change in visibility - warnings may now be invisible in default logging configurations. Consider keeping `LogTrace()?.Warn()` or documenting why the level was downgraded.
```suggestion
				var errorNamePtr = GetMethod<u_errorName>()(status);
				var errorString = errorNamePtr != IntPtr.Zero ? Marshal.PtrToStringUTF8(errorNamePtr) : null;
				var errorMessage = errorString is not null
					? $"{typeof(T).Name} raised a warning code: {errorString}"
					: $"{typeof(T).Name} raised a warning code: 0x{status.ToString("X", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)} (unknown ICU warning)";
				typeof(ICU).Log()?.Warn(errorMessage);
```

Memory allocated with `NativeMemory.AlignedAlloc` is never freed, causing a memory leak. According to the comment on line 128, the buffer needs to remain pinned because `udata_setCommonData` does not copy the buffer. However, the allocated memory should be tracked and freed when the application terminates, or documented that this is intentional behavior for the application lifetime. Consider storing the pointer in a static field with a comment explaining it must remain allocated for the application's lifetime.
```suggestion
				// NOTE: This buffer is intentionally kept for the lifetime of the process because ICU holds a raw pointer
				// to it and expects it to remain valid; it must not be freed while ICU is in use.
				_commonIcuData = NativeMemory.AlignedAlloc((UIntPtr)stream.Length, 16);
				stream.ReadExactly(new Span<byte>(_commonIcuData, (int)stream.Length));
				GetMethod<udata_setCommonData>()((IntPtr)_commonIcuData, out var errorCode);
				CheckErrorCode<udata_setCommonData>(errorCode);
			}
		}

		// Pointer to the ICU common data block passed to udata_setCommonData.
		// This is intentionally never freed because ICU keeps this pointer for the lifetime
		// of the process and expects it to remain valid for as long as ICU is in use.
		private static unsafe void* _commonIcuData;
```

If the reflection calls fail (returning null), the method silently does nothing when `setMethod?.Invoke(...)` is called. This makes it difficult to diagnose initialization failures. Add error handling to throw a descriptive exception if `icuType` or `setMethod` is null, similar to the approach in the generator code which throws `InvalidOperationException` with clear messages.
```suggestion
			if (icuType is null)
			{
				throw new InvalidOperationException("Unable to locate nested type 'UnicodeText.ICU'. Ensure ICU data initializer generation is configured correctly for the Skia target.");
			}

			var setMethod = icuType.GetMethod("SetDataAssembly", BindingFlags.Public | BindingFlags.Static);
			if (setMethod is null)
			{
				throw new InvalidOperationException("Unable to locate method 'SetDataAssembly' on 'UnicodeText.ICU'. Verify that the ICU data initializer type exposes a public static SetDataAssembly method.");
			}

			var assembly = AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies().First(a => a.GetName().Name is { } name && name.StartsWith("SamplesApp", StringComparison.Ordinal) && !name.Equals("SamplesApp.Skia", StringComparison.Ordinal));
			setMethod.Invoke(null, [assembly]);
```

If `stream.Length` exceeds `int.MaxValue`, the cast to `(int)stream.Length` on line 131 will overflow, potentially causing memory corruption. While ICU data files are unlikely to exceed 2GB, add a validation check or use a safe casting method to prevent potential overflow issues.
```suggestion
				var length = stream.Length;
				if (length > int.MaxValue)
				{
					throw new InvalidOperationException("ICU data stream is too large. Maximum supported size is 2GB.");
				}
				var data = NativeMemory.AlignedAlloc((UIntPtr)length, 16);
				var size = (int)length;
				stream.ReadExactly(new Span<byte>(data, size));
```
